    "## Extra credit (3 points). Choose the number of clusters without eyeballing.\n",
    "\n",
    "So far we've picked the number of clusters by looking at plots. The *silhouette score* is one way to measure how well a clustering fits the data. For each point, it compares the average distance to the other points in its own cluster with the average distance to the points in the nearest *other* cluster. A score close to 1 means the point sits snugly inside a well-separated cluster; a score near 0 means it is on the border between two clusters (and a negative score means it's probably in the wrong cluster). `silhouette_score` from `sklearn.metrics` averages this over all the points.\n",
    "\n",
    "Run KMeans for each number of clusters k from 2 through 10, compute the silhouette score for each k, and plot the score versus k. Which k scores best? What happens to the score just after that k, and why do you think that happens?\n",
    "\n",
    "(The silhouette score works best when the clusters are roughly round blobs, as they are here, so it's a useful guide rather than a definitive answer.)"
   ]
